>i
Consciousness is slowly returning...

>
Something inside is telling you to wake up. Part of your mind says "Sunrise approaches." 

Underneath your dream you feel a deep shiver... It is a chime, the bell of a clock-tower. Another follows, and another. You count them, fully awake suddenly, counting them down. Six. Five. Four. Three.

Your eyes close, and your stomach tightens in anticipation. The sheets around you feel very safe.

Two. One.

The first ray of sunlight lances through the slats in the window-blind. It strikes your eyes, and you feel yourself go.




[Press any key]















                       





                         [Please press SPACE to begin.]



Standing on tip-toes.


Scaffold in the City Square
Your head is pulled back, held by the rope pressing on your throat. Your toes pivot on a rickety stool, which shakes as your legs shake. The crowd filling the square are chattering like monkeys, but they are just flits of colour in the corners of your eyes. You cannot look round - instead you gaze straight across the Square, to the great dial of the Clock.

>listen
They talk. A hundred voices, maybe more. They talk of trite things, inane things. They babble.

One voice - familiar - near the front, says deeply: "Well, this way's as good as any."

Slowly, the hand of that great dial swings lower. You are going to hang.

>down
You can almost sense how the rope would feel, tightening around you until life was drained out.

No. Not yet. Not while there is escape.

From somewhere, at the back of the crowd, you hear a woman's voice, sobbing.

>remove rope
You can feel the rope coarse on your throat, and the knot heavy on the nape of your neck. On reflex, hands move up, but are caught by the manacles. There is no way out of here.

"See!" shouts a voice in the crowd. "He's soaked in blood!" He cannot be talking about you, there must be others ready to hang behind and beside, but you cannot turn your head to check.

>examine the clock
The great clock is the centrepiece of the dome of the Palace. It has twenty-four numbers, written in Roman numerals, and only one long hand, shaped like a sword. The dial is twisted round, with the VI - the hour of execution - at the bottom.

The sword on the clock dial swings lower like an executioner's blade. The crowd lets up a great roar! He is going to HANG!

>examine the crowd
You can barely see them, the crowd; the rope keeps your chin up to face the clock. But you can hear them. A huge mass, filling the Square almost to its limit.

The stool rocks suddenly beneath your weight.

>examine square
The central square is about a hundred paces each side, with the two pillars and their statues rising from the centre. Around three sides runs the great Museum, a line of windows, each dark, all the same, like ripples on a sea. They go down as far as the top of the colonnades, in which people will enjoy the shade and drink from the taverna's and the coffee houses. Your scaffold, your home now, is in the cusp of these walls, with your head pointed to the fourth side, where stands the Palace of the Dojo.

A domed building, like a cathedral, its doorway flanked by two green-copper horses reared for the charge and its walls tiled with mosaics. In the very centre, at the very top, is the clock.

The sword-blade is pointing downward, pointing the way to hell. The first chime rings.

>listen
The chimes have begun.

The second chime. In a corner of the square the first hint of dawn appears, as the sun begins to rise.

>wait
Time passes.

The third chime. Through the crowd you hear her sobbing, again.

And then, finally, thank Heaven, it appears. Darkness sways into vision just beside you, a life-line, a life-raft, an escape. But there is little time left. You should go while you have a chance.

>examine darkness
It is darkness, leading away in that other direction. It holds no fear, you have travelled that way so many times before. It is only dark because light cannot travel that way.

A chime again, the sky is brightening in the light of morning. The crowd roars in its excitement. Time to go.

>jump into the darkness
You twitch a movement, and the chair rocks, threatening to topple. The presence on your throat is like a talon.

Fifth. "We are here to hang..." exclaims the executioner from behind you ... 

Enough of this. The crowd bays like dogs and you meet it with a smile. The darkness of the pathway in that other direction is overwhelming and you wonder that they do not see it. Then you go - you leave the scaffold by a quicker route than the steps. Light goes, and comes, and your surroundings have changed.



[Press any key]


You stumble.


Empty Room
The light is dimmer here, the stones are cold. You are in a wide room, Gothic arches rising rib-like to the buttressed roof. Pigeons flit between crevices in the stone, their wing-flaps echo like sharp thunder. Dust spirals in the two slices of light from the crack windows by the roof. Perhaps this room is mainly underground, with the slots at earth-level.

There is but one door north from this vast space, of solid tarred wood, with a heavy lock set into the wood. It is secured.

A small mound of dust and cobwebs has accumulated in one corner.

>blow dust
You close your eyes and blow; the dust billowing back at you in a cloud. You try to wave it away, cannot, and are forced to retreat for a few moments, eyes watering. When they clear, you see an old wine bottle has been uncovered.

Now to sort out your hands, which are still bound behind your back.

>examine wine bottle
An old bottle, with dark red wine visible behind the green glass. The label is too old to read, and maybe never said anything to start with.

You hear a noise, from behind the door. Footsteps. Followed by a scraping in the lock, of a key being removed.

>kick the bottle
You flail at the bottle, sending it flying toward the wall to shatter in a fountain of thick wine with glass-splinter foam. It soaks over your legs, and leaks amongst the stones.

At the sound of the smash, the pigeons take fright. The birds swoop out of the thin windows in a panic, and out onto the street.

You hear a man cursing from the street, saying: "The birds, you see, Miss? These cursed birds fly up at the slightest thing, I hate them!"

"Yes, there are quite - eek - a number of these creatures!" exclaims the woman. "Something must have startled them down there."

The voices come again from the street above. "Now, Antonio. I will look after this. Before I go, then, why don't you just check the door is securely locked?"

"Certainly," comes a man's reply. "I wouldn't want any of those cursed birds flying out."

Birds stream in and out of the windows, shredding the light.

>rub ropes on glass
Slowly, you align the glass and the ropes. They slip - you try again. It's difficult, fumbling work, and each time you get through one strand the glass slips and you have to readjust it. Eventually, you sever the width of the rope - but the knot does not come free, it is so tangled, and the glass slips of the threads. You pause with ropes half cut.

You hear footsteps in the passage outside the door, and at the same time one beam of light passes out, then appears once more, as something falls to the floor through the window slot. It is a key - a heavy iron key!

Birds stream in and out of the windows, shredding the light.

>again
Again, you painstakingly angle the glass to the fibres, and begin the slow process of chafing. One by one the strands around your wrist snap, and now the tension eases; until finally the rope knot slithers to the floor.

Gratefully you rub your hands together, and notice for the first time - you are wearing a gold ring.

The door rattles, someone is testing that it is locked. And the light dims again, and something floats to ground. Then footsteps leave the passage outside the door, and Antonio says "It's all closed there, Miss."

"Good," she replies, "Watch out for those birds," and you hear her footsteps receding, leaving you alone.

Birds stream in and out of the windows, shredding the light.

>examine ring
A golden ring, stamped with the Seal of Rome. You do not remember acquiring it; it must have been won gambling in the inn last night, or somesuch. It is probably quite precious.

The birds settle down, and finally come back to roost.

You hear the guard go back up to the street, cursing "Damned birds."

>read note
The note is stained deep red with wine, and reads:

They will be after you now, and me. Get out of the city as soon as you can. Good luck. (Antonio is scared of the birds, if that is any help.)

The hand - a woman's - is unfamiliar, and whoever your assistant is they have remained anonymous.

>i
Notably, you are carrying:
  a golden signet ring around your finger

>take key
Taken.

>unlock the door
(with the solid iron key)
The heavy lock turns with a thunk.

>n
(opening the solid wooden door first)
The door is heavy, but swings slowly open.

Time to find out where you are, and how you can leave. You don't want to return to the scaffold, but you will have little choice if they find you.

Stone Passage
The stones are grey, and dim with no light to help your way. You fumble for the walls in dotty blackness, just making out the heavy door to the south and the rough-cut steps which lead away upwards.

At the top of the steps is a closed hatch.

>close hatch
The hatch has no keyhole, but there is a metal hoop on this side and a bracket in the wall. They don't connect, but could be used to fix the hatch shut if you had a cross-beam.

>lock it
The hatch has no keyhole, but there is a metal hoop on this side and a bracket in the wall. They don't connect, but could be used to fix the hatch shut if you had a cross-beam.

>i
Notably, you are carrying:
  a solid iron key
  a golden signet ring around your finger

>put key through hoop
You ram the key through the hoop so that the bulky floret sticks into the bracket, fixing it shut.

>s

Empty Room
The light is dimmer here, the stones are cold. You are in a wide room, Gothic arches rising rib-like to the buttressed roof. Pigeons flit between crevices in the stone, their wing-flaps echo like sharp thunder. Dust spirals in the two slices of light from the crack windows by the roof. Perhaps this room is mainly underground, with the slots at earth-level.

There is but one door north from this vast space, of solid tarred wood, with a heavy lock set into the wood. It is open.

There is a red puddle on the floor, like blood, amongst shards of broken green bottle-glass.

You can also see a scribbled note and some thick rope here.

>take rope
Taken.

>throw rope at pigeons
Like a cloud the pigeons all rise from your missile.

The birds swoop out of the thin windows in a panic, and out onto the street. You hear a man yelping, and a banging on the hatchway. Then footsteps, running away down the alley.

Birds stream in and out of the windows, shredding the light.

>n

Stone Passage
The stones are grey, and dim with no light to help your way. You fumble for the walls in dotty blackness, just making out the heavy door to the south and the rough-cut steps which lead away upwards.

At the top of the steps is a closed hatch, sealed shut with the solid iron key.

>take key
You slip out the key, and free the hatch.

>u. s. ne
(opening the hatch first)
You open the hatch.

You make your way slowly up the stairs, careful not to slip on their worn surface.

Quiet Venetian Street
The alleyway is a typical shaded Venetian street, its walls barely a shoulder-width apart, rising three or four stories high on either side. The plaster is cream, and a few thin wiry balconies emerge on either side, dotted with flowers, looking small and crumpled in the shade.

The alley winds south to northeast, around the edges of the buildings deeper into the maze of Venice. In the pit of the alley's elbow, the southeast wall, there is a dark opening into which steps descend.

A great flock of birds eddies around in the tight space of the alley.

A metal sign on one wall reads: "Piazza St. Marco" and points along the alley southwards.

A little way south of the bend, at the bottom of the eastern wall, are two thin slits in the stone.

The birds settle down, and finally go back to roost.
You follow the alleyway along, until suddenly the buildings either side cease and you find yourself under a colonnade in the central Square of the City.

Piazza St. Marco
The square is magnificent - a wide expanse in which people mill, and enjoy the sunshine. All that surrounds them is gigantic, from the colossal pillars in the centre holding the statues of the city, to the wall of the Museum with its myriad windows running the perimeter, to the Palace itself, which stands to the east.

The thin alley-mouths dot the corners, under the colonnades, tucked away. Pigeons fly back and forth, smaller still than the people.

Workmen swarm around the scaffold at the far west of the square, presumably taking it down.

The Piazza is full of wandering noblemen, and cluttered with couples, bazaars, merchants, Guardsmen... you duck quickly back into your alleyway, and the safety of the shade.

Quiet Venetian Street
The alleyway is a typical shaded Venetian street, its walls barely a shoulder-width apart, rising three or four stories high on either side. The plaster is cream, and a few thin wiry balconies emerge on either side, dotted with flowers, looking small and crumpled in the shade.

The alley winds south to northeast, around the edges of the buildings deeper into the maze of Venice. In the pit of the alley's elbow, the southeast wall, there is a dark opening into which steps descend.

A metal sign on one wall reads: "Piazza St. Marco" and points along the alley southwards.

A little way south of the bend, at the bottom of the eastern wall, are two thin slits in the stone.
You pace along the street, careful to keep your feet in check, prevent them from bursting into flight. The City Guard will be looking for you, after your disappearance from the scaffold. Best look inconspicuous.

Campo St. Maria Formosa
Here the alley opens quite suddenly into a wide plaza, with a fountain in its centre and a high church at the eastern end. A myriad of passages and streets, some as thin as the one from the southwest which you entered by, others wider yet more twisting, lead off in several directions.

A metal sign on one wall reads: "Campo St. Maria Formosa".

A young woman stands by the fountain, bathing her face.

The woman tosses something into the fountain, and crosses herself.

>examine woman
A young girl, maybe twenty-three, dressed in a long brown dress of some coarse material. Her hair is long and raven-black, and she is perched on the fountain bowl, idling away time.

Water splashes high from the fountain casting rainbows in the light.

>talk to woman
You call to the woman, who turns around quickly. Her eyes fall to your trouser-legs, soaked in red wine, and she shrieks; leaving the plaza and fleeing along one of the streets.

Water splashes high from the fountain casting rainbows in the light.

>drink from fountain
You take several long gulps of the cool water, not realising how thirsty you feel until it passes your lips.

>enter the church
You take quick steps across the plaza, and into the shade of the church. Your eyes take a moment to adjust to the gloom. Perhaps this place can save you. If you could claim the Sanctuary of the Cross...

Church
Air is still here, resting between the candelabras on either wall, bowed before the altar at the far end. Each wall is awash with paintings; saints and saviours grace every surface in vivid colours and swirling movement.

The pews are empty, line after line waiting for worshippers. Light streams in through the great doorway to the west, just picking out the vestry archway to the south.

By the doorway is a ceramic bowl, for alms.

By the altar stands a man in a long black robe - the priest.

>talk to the priest
"Please. We are but a poor ministry," he says before you so much as draw breath, without even turning round.

>i
Notably, you are carrying:
  a solid iron key
  a golden signet ring around your finger

>remove ring
You slip the ring off.

>put ring in alms bowl
You drop in your gift, and the priest nods slowly. "What is your problem, my son?" he asks, still facing away.

>talk to priest
"Father!" you cry, and the man turns slowly round. He is holding a book in one hand, his face a long mask. "Father, I claim Sanctuary!"

"What from, my son?" he asks, and with a flush you remember the wine that had scared the girl outside.

"I am being hunted by the City Guard," you reply simply.

"What have you done?"

"Nothing," you reply. "I was to be hanged this morning, but I escaped."

"Why were you to be hanged?"

You fall silent for a moment. It is hard to explain.

>again
"I don't know," you begin. "You see... well, sometimes I move in a direction that other people can't take. They say the shortest distance between two points is a straight line; but that's not true. It's a curious thing: I don't understand it." The other's face is impassive. Nonetheless, you continue.

"And every morning, it pulls me away. I can't control where it takes me - this morning it took me to the scaffold." You of course do not mention your career. It isn't relevant now.

The priest nods. "I see." He turns his head around to gaze at the altar for a moment, then back. "There are many routes to the hangman's noose. And there are many who feel they did not control their path. I offer you the best sanctuary I can." With that, he walks away, along the line of pews south to the vestry, beckoning that you should follow.

>sit
You take a seat on the wood.

>pray
You bow your head in prayer. No thoughts come to you though, nothing but the confusion always engendered by travelling through the dark. Where are you? Some days are simple - aside from the morning you ignore the darkness as it hovers by your eyes. But some days you do not have that choice.

Your eyes stare, still open, at the floor of the church.  The pew where you sit is built upon a grave-stone, the inscription in Latin, a tongue you know not. The name reads "W. DeLosa" - by coincidence, your own name - and the dates of his life: "21st June, 1533 - 24th June 1561". You lift your head once more, feeling no peace.

>stand
You stand.

Church
Air is still here, resting between the candelabras on either wall, bowed before the altar at the far end. Each wall is awash with paintings; saints and saviours grace every surface in vivid colours and swirling movement.

The pews are empty, line after line waiting for worshippers. Light streams in through the great doorway to the west, just picking out the vestry archway to the south.

By the doorway is a ceramic bowl, for alms.

>follow priest
You follow the priest along the line of benches and into the shade of the vestry.

Vestry
The vestry of the church is a modest affair, still candle-lit stone, hung with one large and very beautiful tapestry. The priest's desk takes up most of the floor space, covered in scrolls of paper, a selection of quill pens, with a very deep inkwell in one corner. This is before the window, so that the light streams over his shoulders from where he sits, half-blinding you.

The door shuts quietly behind you, the cold tip of a knife appears on your back. Then a voice snaps: "Where is it?"

The priest stands, and leaves the room. A moment later, he returns and gives something to the man behind you, who is careful not to let you turn. "He paid it in alms," the vicar says.

"A last attempt to save his soul," the man laughs. "Excellent. Well," and he turns you around, and you recognise his face, from the scaffold this morning: it is the Captain of the Guard. "I am told you are to be hanged."

>talk to captain
"I have claimed sanctuary!" you exclaim, turning in panic to the priest, who slowly, like wind in a high tree, shakes his head.

"The Church is built upon Confession, and you would not confess," the priest says gently. "So this is the only sanctuary I can give."

"But I have done nothing!"

They do not listen. Your hands are secured once more behind your back, and you are led away through the Venetian streets, the Captain of the Guard behind you with knifepoint at your spine.

Venetian Streets
You walk through streets and winding alleys, avoiding the canals where the crowds will be. The Captain has one hand on the manacles around your hands, and the sharp tip of metal is ever present on your back. Tall buildings rise impassive on either side, cutting out the sun, and you wonder if you shall ever see it again.

But though it is dark, it is never dark enough for you to leave.

"We found you quite quickly," remarks the Captain under his breath, "because one of your friends tipped us off. Fancy that? I thought you would have the protection of the Denizen, but it seems your time has come." He laughs. "And I had heard you were for our side."

>run
You pull away sharply, but the Captain draws you back sharper still, onto the point of his blade. "No, no, you're not going anywhere," he drawls in your ear. "Keep walking."

A merchant passes you, and the Captain draws closer behind, to shield your bonds and the dagger he holds. The man nods his hello, and is gone.

>examine me
You are the same person you have always been. You feel the same way. The legs of your trousers are splattered red.

You cannot look in your pockets. All that you know you have is:
  a pair of manacles (binding your wrists together behind your back)

You walk further.

>cut manacles on dagger
You raise the manacles slowly so that the metal rubs along the blade, but they are well made - it will take an age to be free this way. And the Captain knows it.

With an almost-imperceptible shimmer, your companion darkness appears beside you.

>talk to captain
"Who in the Resistance turned me in?" you demand.

"Wouldn't you like to know? But I won't say."

"What does it matter? If I'm to hang!"

The Captain considers this. "Yes, you're right, I suppose. I still won't tell you though. It's someone high up, one of your leaders I suppose. We know them all - the leaders - well enough. She said you'd be in the area, though not exactly where. Giving you a chance, I suppose."

The alley makes an elbow bend, you follow it around.

>g. g
"I'm not in the Resistance," you say.

"Really?" the Captain replies, disinterested. "We both know that's not true. Now, I thought you were working for the Denizen..."

"No," you reply. "But I'm an assassin, it's a profession, nothing more. I kill for whoever pays me to. I'm no different, Captain, from you."

"You expect me to believe that? Look at you. Setting yourself up there for years. Maybe the Denizen herself was the target?"

You leave an alley and frog-march across a wide plaza, in which several groups of people lounge, drinking and enjoying the sunshine. None pays any attention, even though you half-recognise some from the crowd this morning; and then the shade falls again and you are in another street, alone with the Captain once more.

>enter the darkness
A quick side-step into the dark, far too fast for the Captain's eyes, and no doubt your smile fades last as you disappear from his sight, removed.



[Press any key]


You find yourself in magnificent surroundings, and it takes a moment to recognise the Grand Staircase in the Palace of the Dojo.


Grand Staircase
This staircase is the only part of the Palace the mere natives of Venice ever see, a grand mouth leading up to the public rooms and offices of the Dojo's officials. Its stairs are wide, polished marble, and the roof curves low overhead, near so as to intimidate as much as bring into view the wonderful paintings that cover its surface. Some of the finest art of Venice is here, in the little boxes that run up the length of this great passage.

At the top of the stairs a man emerges from the Denizen's room, grinning widely. He rushes down the steps past you, exclaiming: "They signed me up! I was going to hang tomorrow, but they've signed me up! I'm in the Army!" And he leaps out into the Square.

The thought passes across your head: That would be a way out of this.

>examine steps
The stone steps are a light brown colour, and polished such that the sunlight makes needle-points on them.

>i
Notably, you are carrying:
  a room key

>x key
The key to your room.

>drop it
You wouldn't want to lose your door key.

>go upstairs

At the Top of the Grand Staircase
You stand at the top of the wide, magnificent staircase, facing a large window with a view of a quiet canal. There are two doors here, to the north and south, which make up the public offices of the Palace. According to their signs, the former is the Denizen's chamber, whilst the other leads to the office of one Giuseppe Florantine. Both are closed.

Just below the window is a marble pedestal, on which is a large volume - the appointments book.

Something makes you look, hard, at Florantine's door, though you can't think what it is.

>examine florantine's door
The door to this room is heavy wood, with a large brass keyhole. A plaque by the side reads: "OFFICE OF G. Florantine, AIDE TO THE DENIZEN".

>knock on denizen's door
You hesitate - you ought to sign the book first.

>sign book
With an automatic movement, you sign the book.

>n
You knock smartly, and enter.

Chamber of the Denizen
This room is enormous - it stretches from the doorway south right back along the length of this section of the Palace, so that the large window at the far end looks out over the Square. It would have a clear view of the scaffold, if it were there, and you tense.

By the door the room is mostly empty but for an enchanting rug, whereas the windowed end is a mess of cabinets and shelves, glass display-cases and stools. The Denizen herself sits behind a wide desk, reclining back in her chair, watching you.

On one wall is a clock, a replica of that outside.

The Denizen nods up at you. "Close the door," she commands. You do so. "Take a seat."

>sit
You take your seat.

The Denizen spreads her hand on the desk. "Exactly what is it you require?"

>examine denizen
The Denizen is a tall woman, just reaching the first stages of middle age but carrying herself as though twenty. She is seated, but her broad shoulders and firm eyes dominate the room - though they are not as broad as your own, just as she is not tall as you. Her features are notably Roman; brows heavy, nose angular. She wears robes of State, and wears them with ease; her expression is one of tolerant authority.

She drums her fingers on the table.

>talk to her
You pause. "I would like to join the Army," you say.

The Denizen smiles beatifically. "Excellent, my good man. I need you to sign a form to that effect. Go and get one from my aide."

>g
"Can I not join without?" you ask. She looks at you curiously.

"Why?"

You do not reply.

You glance down at the desk, and notice suddenly your ring, lying by the candle. It must have slid there from your finger.

>examine the candle
A candle as thick as your wrist, made of thick sealing wax.

>take it
The presence of authority is somewhat intimidating, so you refrain from fiddling with her things.

>take ring
You retrieve your ring, and slide it on.

>stand
"Return here with it then," she says easily. You stand.

Chamber of the Denizen
This room is enormous - it stretches from the doorway south right back along the length of this section of the Palace, so that the large window at the far end looks out over the Square. It would have a clear view of the scaffold, if it were there, and you tense.

By the door the room is mostly empty but for an enchanting rug, whereas the windowed end is a mess of cabinets and shelves, glass display-cases and stools. The Denizen herself sits behind a wide desk, reclining back in her chair, watching you.

On one wall is a clock, a replica of that outside.

>s. s
You open the door, and go through.

At the Top of the Grand Staircase
You stand at the top of the wide, magnificent staircase, facing a large window with a view of a quiet canal. There are two doors here, to the north and south, which make up the public offices of the Palace. According to their signs, the former is the Denizen's chamber, whilst the other leads to the office of one Giuseppe Florantine. The aide's door is closed.

Just below the window is a marble pedestal, on which is a large volume - the appointments book.

Something makes you look, hard, at Florantine's door, though you can't think what it is.

You close the Denizen's door behind you.
You knock smartly, and enter.

"Yes, hello?" asks a voice from inside, as you enter. "How can I help you?"

Office of Giuseppe Florantine
The aide's office is simple enough - cabinets line one wall, and a large desk fills the centre of the room, its piles of paper extremely organised. The aide himself is seated in a deep chair behind, looking up at you expectantly.

Behind you, the door is still open.

Giuseppe stands quickly, and shuts the door. "I keep all discussion private," he remarks quickly.

>talk to giuseppe
(Giuseppe Florantine)
"I would like to join the Army."

Giuseppe smiles. "Excellent! We need all the men we can. Do you have a criminal record?"

>examine him
A rather weasly man, perhaps a little older than you, in his early thirties. His clothing is simple and dark, his expression one of petulance. There is something about him that just makes you want to hit him heavily in the face; it's a curious feeling, and you've no idea why. He sits in his deep chair, staring right at you.

The aide looks at you like a mole. "I need to know about your criminal history, sir."

>hit him
A visualisation of murdering this man passes across the inside of your eyes, but externally you merely smile. At least, you think, it would not be difficult if it were required.

>talk to him
"Yes. I've been an assassin for eight years now," you reply finally.

The aide blanches, then his smile returns. "Well, I should think the Army will be happy to have you then, sir! Here..." and he scrabbles on his desk for a moment before handing you a piece of paper, "is a form. Give that over to the Denizen, across the hall. Good luck, sir!"

>x form
A simple enough form - lines for your name, and nationality, and for your signature, the Denizen's and that of her aide. Giuseppe's rat-like scrawl is already down, as is a brief line detailing your profession.

"Through that way, sir," Giuseppe adds helpfully, indicating the only door.

>n
You open the door, and go through.

At the Top of the Grand Staircase
You stand at the top of the wide, magnificent staircase, facing a large window with a view of a quiet canal. There are two doors here, to the north and south, which make up the public offices of the Palace. According to their signs, the former is the Denizen's chamber, whilst the other leads to the office of one Giuseppe Florantine. The Denizen's door is closed, her aide's is open.

Something makes you look, hard, at Florantine's door, though you can't think what it is.

Just below the window is a marble pedestal, on which is a large volume - the appointments book.

The aide's door is quickly shut.

>sign form
You sign the form using the pen on the appointment book.

>g
You already have.

>n
You knock smartly, and enter.

Chamber of the Denizen
This room is enormous - it stretches from the doorway south right back along the length of this section of the Palace, so that the large window at the far end looks out over the Square. It would have a clear view of the scaffold, if it were there, and you tense.

By the door the room is mostly empty but for an enchanting rug, whereas the windowed end is a mess of cabinets and shelves, glass display-cases and stools. The Denizen herself sits behind a wide desk, reclining back in her chair, watching you.

On one wall is a clock, a replica of that outside.

"You again," booms the Denizen. "Close that door, sit down." You do so.

>give form to her
She takes the form and scans over it quickly. "You've signed it already? You are presumptuous, aren't you?" She laughs. "Good, very good," she nods to herself. Then, with quill poised: "A criminal, are you?" looking up at you. "What would you bring to the army then?"

>talk to her
"I'm a skilled assassin," you say.

"We have plenty of those," she replies smoothly. "What do you bring?"

>g
"I have an ability they do not have," you begin. "You see: I can move quicker than they can. There are quicker routes between places than most people see."

"Explain," replies the Denizen, her brow raised.

"Just that. I can move between places far quicker than other people. There is this other direction which becomes open to me. When it does - why, I could step to Rome as easily as across the Square outside." The Denizen is looking at you curiously, understandably, with her pen still an inch above the paper.

"Show me," she says simply.

"Well?" the Denizen asks quietly.

>go
There is no darkness, there is no shorter route from here.

>close eyes
You close your eyes briefly, in memory of all that you have lost. It is darkness there, but not darkness enough. Your eyes reopen, refocussing to your task.

>talk to her
You frown. "Right now - I cannot. But just this morning," you begin, quickly, so as not to catch yourself before you say, "I did just this. I was to be hanged - I was there, on the scaffold, in the Square; and just as the clock chimed the fifth chime I left it, stepped away."

"How did you get to the scaffold?" the Denizen asks. "Did a victim scream out your name?"

"No," I reply. "Each morning, at 6am, I cannot help but move that way. It takes hold of me. I appeared there, noose around my neck."

The Denizen nods, and puts her quill down on the table. "Well, you sound reliable," she says finally. "A man who may well not turn up for training, his only excuse being that he was in Rome."

"Is that it, then?" she asks.

>g
"But will you take me, all the same?" you persist, and the Denizen smiles, collecting up her quill.

"Yes, I think we shall. We have use for men, regardless." With a quick flourish she signs the document and puts a cross by the signature you've already so helpfully provided. "Well, begone my friend. You have your pardons, for whatever your crimes. Return here in three days time, on the 26th, and you shall have your uniform." She reaches out to shake your hand, then waves for you to go.

>take form
The form is hers now, as is your future.

>x it
A simple enough form - lines for your name, and nationality, and for your signature, the Denizen's and that of her aide. Giuseppe's rat-like scrawl is already down, as is a brief line detailing your profession. Your mark has been made. The Denizen's precise mark is there too.

>s. w. d. nw
The Denizen nods to you. You open the door, and go through.

At the Top of the Grand Staircase
You stand at the top of the wide, magnificent staircase, facing a large window with a view of a quiet canal. There are two doors here, to the north and south, which make up the public offices of the Palace. According to their signs, the former is the Denizen's chamber, whilst the other leads to the office of one Giuseppe Florantine. The aide's door is closed.

Something makes you look, hard, at Florantine's door, though you can't think what it is.

Just below the window is a marble pedestal, on which is a large volume - the appointments book.

You close the Denizen's door behind you.
You smile, your immunity guaranteed by the Denizen's hand, and hop down the stairs toward the Square.

Grand Staircase
This staircase is the only part of the Palace the mere natives of Venice ever see, a grand mouth leading up to the public rooms and offices of the Dojo's officials. Its stairs are wide, polished marble, and the roof curves low overhead, near so as to intimidate as much as bring into view the wonderful paintings that cover its surface. Some of the finest art of Venice is here, in the little boxes that run up the length of this great passage.

From the top of the staircase you see the Denizen emerge, heading to the aide's room. You watch as her eyes catch the appointment book - and she curses: "Idiot!" Tearing out a page, she returns to her own.
You step out into the sunshine.

Piazza St. Marco
The square is magnificent - a wide expanse in which people mill, and enjoy the sunshine. All that surrounds them is gigantic, from the colossal pillars in the centre holding the statues of the city, to the wall of the Museum with its myriad windows running the perimeter, to the Palace itself, which stands to the east.

The thin alley-mouths dot the corners, under the colonnades, tucked away. Pigeons fly back and forth, smaller still than the people. The scaffold has been taken down.
A woman comes running up to you, the tips of her blonde hair streaming in the wind, the rest restained under a hat. "There you are," she hisses in your ear, as she slips an arm through yours, and hurries you off toward an alleyway. "I waited for you, like you told me to, yesterday evening? I hope you got it all done. I imagine now you feel the need to relax a little - we could have a drink?" she giggles. Who is this woman?

Quiet Venetian Street
The alleyway is a typical shaded Venetian street, its walls barely a shoulder-width apart, rising three or four stories high on either side. The plaster is cream, and a few thin wiry balconies emerge on either side, dotted with flowers, looking small and crumpled in the shade.

The alley winds south to northeast, around the edges of the buildings deeper into the maze of Venice. In the pit of the alley's elbow, the southeast wall, there is a dark opening into which steps descend.

A metal sign on one wall reads: "Piazza St. Marco" and points along the alley southwards.

Looped around your arm is the pretty girl.

A little way south of the bend, at the bottom of the eastern wall, are two thin slits in the stone.

"The meeting-point," she whispers quietly. "Just down there," and she points to the passage downwards.

>talk to girl
"Where are you taking me?"

She giggles. "We'd best meet the others first, they'll want to know how it went."

"But I.."

"And then," she interrupts. "We can go somewhere else, maybe."

The young woman drapes her other hand on your shoulder. "I suppose we should go," she says slowly, not going anywhere at all.

>kiss her
You catch her around the waist, and give her a long, firm kiss. She relaxes quite happily into your arms, and the moment lasts a good while, the sun on your faces, before you finally pull away. "Darling," she whispers, then, regretfully: "We'd better hurry."

"It's a beautiful little alley, isn't it?" she says quietly.

"I suppose," you reply.

>d
The woman catches your arm as you make for the passage. "Can I ask you," she says, and you look almost certainly with a look of dread. "I'm sorry but... did you really mean it? What you said last night?"

The girl looks at you seriously.

>kiss her
You catch her around the waist, and give her a long, firm kiss. She relaxes quite happily into your arms, and the moment lasts a good while, the sun on your faces, before you finally pull away. "Darling," she whispers. "We'd best go." And she takes your hand, leading you down the stairs.

Stone Passage
The passage is well-lit now, and filled with shadowy characters, three or four Resistance members including the one who originally hired you. There is tension in the air, and they all look up quite gratefully when you enter, with the woman.

At the top of the steps is an open hatch which hangs downwards.

"Here he is, Father," the girl exclaims to the group. "He's done it!"

The old man you recognised nods to her. "Is this true? You've done your job?"

>talk to man
"No," you reply finally, a little irritated.

"What? Francesca?" He turns to the girl, but you reply.
   
"If you had given me a target, I would have done it. But I've heard nothing from you."

Francesca looks confused; the others are looking at her. "But I told you - I sent a note, to your room."

"Francesca, did you get the right room?" asks a thin voice at the back.

"No, I'm sure she did," you reply. "I have not been back to my room yet."

Francesca looks sharply at you, and suddenly her face constricts. "I see. I sent it, well, early this morning. Before anyone was really awake." She steps nearer, to hiss. "But I suppose, if you weren't in your bed last night, you wouldn't have got it." She looks at her Father, suddenly not at you. "He was in the Palace now, though. He was there a while."

"He's a traitor," mutters a voice from the back.

>g
"That's true enough," you reply slowly, unwilling. "I was in the Palace."

The man notices the ring on your finger. "Gift from a friend?" he asks, not you, but the group. Then, turning back, so that your eyes meet: "Listen, friend. We need some time to decide what to do from here. I'm sure you understand." Francesca moves to stand behind him, not looking at you. Her eyes are wet. "We will deal with you later."

"What do you mean?" you ask, stupidly. They reply with strong ropes tied around your wrists; you protest, explaining how you had left your room suddenly, how you had not yet returned. But Francesca speaks over your voice, pronouncing you untrustworthy, a spy, a traitor, a villain. The group is quickly in agreement - this first, whilst they decide what to do next.

The door opens, and you are pushed through, stumbling, into the wine cellar for the second time in one day. "You know his reputation," Francesca says, and after a murmur they loop the rope once around the door handle and tie it fast. A thin voice from the back apologises, then the door is slammed shut.

Empty Room
The light is dimmer here, the stones are cold. You are in a wide room, Gothic arches rising rib-like to the buttressed roof.  The pigeons have gone now, perhaps out into the city to feed, leaving the room quiet, and you can make little of the room's walls out in the dim light.

There is but one door north from this vast space, of solid tarred wood, with a heavy lock set into the wood. It is secured.

And you are secured to it.

>remove ropes
The knot would be too complex for you to untie, even if you could see it.

You look straight away for the broken glass, to free yourself again. But the floor has been swept clean, it is too dark to look for more, you are tied short to the door... Quickly, you give up and resign yourself to waiting. There is no escape this time - no key, nothing to undo the ropes with.

"You can't tie him up! What's he going to do to us?" whimpers a voice through the door.

>open door
You could hardly pull a door that heavy with your teeth.

You wait a long while, voices waxing and waning outside the door. The only one you recognise is that of Francesca, who is waiting, it seems. Eventually she says: "I'm going for a walk, to stretch my legs." You hear her footsteps disappear down the alley.

Sunlight is gradually seeping through the windows.

>x light
There is no light, the sun is in the wrong part of the sky now.

Footsteps approach along the alley, and into the passage. You hear a weasly voice say: "What the hell is this?"

He is met a long silence, until the leader replies. "What are you doing here?"

"One of your clowns left this," and paper rustles. "Do you want me hanged?"

You are developing a headache.

>wait
Time passes.

Suddenly the door swings open, throwing you forwards, and people enter. You hear voices talking, but your headache has sharply worsened and you can barely see. Someone says "Well, DeLosa, here he is! All yours!" Someone else asks for Francesca, but she is not yet back. Then the barrage in your head gets worse and you can no longer hear, except for the echo of voices, more voices, one of them your own.

You feel dragged and pulled, by your own feet perhaps: you are staggering and nauseous. Then, with a wash of gratitude you see that the darkness has boiled up around like a fog and with a release from the pounding you slide.


[Press any key]


Moving forward.


Venetian Streets
Streets and alleys once more. You are walking along, and it takes a moment to note the knife point in your spine, your hands secure behind your back, and the Captain of the Guard's breath upon your neck.

No time has passed, it seems, since you were here last.

It is a wonder the Captain can remember his route amongst the spagehetti of alleys.

