>kiss her
You can't see "her" (Francesca) at the moment.

>talk to woman
You can't see any such thing.

>stand
You can barely put half an arm through; not even that with wrists in manacles. There is no escape.

A tall figure quietly approaches your cell, dressed in a cowled cape that shields their face. "I'm sorry, my friend," she says - a woman with a deep voice.

It is the Denizen.

>take money
You can't see any such thing.

>talk to her
"Why are you here?"

"Farewells."

>give coin to her
You can't see any such thing.

>talk to her
"I can escape, you know. At any time."

"Moving in a different direction?" She pauses, in silence, then says slowly: "I do not think I understand this. Perhaps you have been in the Resistance too long, my friend."

>talk to her
"Help me escape," you plead. "They will hang me."

"I will hang you," she replies quietly. She leans forward, and you catch the glint of eyes. "They are baiting me, you see. Perhaps they know the truth about you? If they do not, then your execution will strike them quite a blow. If they do - and it seems to me they must - that will spread the seeds of doubt. I have to be seen to act, do I not? If I let you live, that is a victory for them. That undoes all your work. It gives them that confidence. If I let you hang, they will worry that maybe they have done the wrong thing. And they will fear me more."

>look

Cell, Night
The bars of your cell form one wall, the remaining three are solid rock, broken only by the small barred window. There is no bed, there is no bucket. There is no escape, but for you there is hope. You sit and wait for that other direction to become available. Time takes a long time to pass as you wait.

Beyond the bars of your cell a tall cowled figure hovers like an apparition.

"You will be an example of the efficiency of the Denizen. And perhaps they will be more careful of their other members, less inclined to throw them away so flippantly - and so you see, you secure all our other men. I think it fair that you should know: you will be helping the cause tomorrow."

>take note
You can't see any such thing.

>dress
Your hands are firmly pinioned behind your back by the manacles.

"And you have made too many mistakes along the way. This is the last of them." Her voice is like steel. "How else can I be rid of you? I cannot have you resenting me, selling out. This is the best way for us. If you were seen to live in the Resistance, you must be seen to die thus."

>read note
(in the cowled woman)
She is on the other side of the bars.

"Maybe you prefer it this way, too? Did you sell me out, Seppe?"

She sighs. "No matter. Ultimately, you are expendable. You made too many mistakes." And with that, and a shake of the hood, she turns and is gone.

>open blind
You can't see any such thing.

>read note
What do you want to read in?

