You can't see any such thing.

>listen
You hear nothing unexpected.

....The night wears on.....

>out
You can barely put half an arm through; not even that with wrists in manacles. There is no escape.

Quite suddenly, in the middle of the night, they feed you - hard bread, and a earthernware cup of water, too solid to break, too light to be a weapon. You wolf the meal, which quenches your hunger a little, and the remains are removed.

>close door
Your hands are firmly pinioned behind your back by the manacles.

....The night wears on.....

>s. d
You can barely put half an arm through; not even that with wrists in manacles. There is no escape.

...and then morning arrives, very early morning; you can hear it in the movements of the guards and the opening of cells as people are collected for the scaffold. This morning, only one cell is opened, and it is yours, and you are led away.

The darkness has still not returned.

You are led slowly through passages of the Guard-house to the Bridge of Sighs, though you barely notice you are over water through the pin-prick windows. There are uniformed men in front and behind, two holding your arms so firmly that when you are tired, you lift your feet and they carry you instead. And then, through the side of the Dojo's Palace to emerge blinking into the sunlight of the Square. The crowd hollers at your entrance.

The procession troops to the scaffold, and the Captain emerges from its shade to lead up you up the final steps. Looking around frantically, you are helped onto the bent wooden stool, once again, and the noose is slid around your neck. Just as he reaches to pull down the knot - there it is. That doorway has opened. Flushed with victory, ready to scream your defiance to the crowd, you step through and you are gone...



[Press any key]


Under your back feels soft, and for a moment your eyes closed from weariness. Soft light comes through the blind. You are back - every morning the same: you always return eventually.


Bedroom (on the bed)
Your room - in the inn, above one of the smaller canals, in heart of the ghetto area of the city, as far from the Hanging Square as you could hope. The window looks out over the canal, but the blind is closed now, and only a little light leaks through. You lie on your bed, pushed up against one wall, facing the door which leads west. It is a simple room, just a sink and chair for furniture, but secure all the same.

The door stands closed and locked.

A sink stands against one wall. The bowl is empty.

On the chair are a coin and a pile of clothing.

You are lying on the bed, the sheets awry as though you had just woken from a nightmare. And you are not alone.
You rise from the bed, and from here you see that a slip of paper has been pushed underneath your door.

The only door is west.

>wait
Time passes.

>stand
You are standing.

>open blind
You throw back the shutter, and let in the daylight.

Light falls across the woman's eyes, and she stirs awake. Eyelids flicker open to see you. "Hello again," she whispers quietly.

It takes a moment for your eyes to fix her face - and then with a shudder you realise she is the woman you saw earlier today, from the fountain. How could she be here? Does she travel through the darkness too?

The woman smiles at you, waiting for something.

From the light outside, it must be about ten o'clock. Presumably you fell asleep again. Understandable!

>kiss her
She puts a hand on your chest, holding you away. "If you want to find me again this evening, please do," she laughs.

>talk to woman
"Hello again," you reply. "I'm surprised. I didn't know there were others who could travel like I do."

She laughs, looking a little perplexed, but only says "Neither did I. One gold coin." You blink in surprise.

You have some money somewhere - there, on the chair.

>take money
Taken.

"Come on, sleepy-head," she says gently.

>give coin to her
She accepts the coin with a nod. "It has been a pleasure." With that she stands smoothly from the bed, and removes a tunic from the chair, slipping it over her head. Though embarassed, you find your eyes following her movements.

She unlocks the door and tosses the key onto the bed.

>talk to her
"Can you not leave by travelling through the dark?"

"But it's morning!" she exclaims, confused.

The woman opens the door, about to leave.

>dress
You are already fully clothed. You tend to sleep this way, in case of emergency.

The lady leaves with a nod to you, closing the door behind her.

>open door
You open the door.

>out
You'd best read your note. It should be your instructions, that's what the girl said.

>read note
A note, neatly folded and written in a woman's handwriting - the same as that other note, in fact! It reads:

Our traitor is Giuseppe Florantine, aide to the Denizen. You may dispatch him any way you choose. I would have told you in person, and joined you in bed, but the door was locked. I did not wish to wake you. Please take care. And destroy this note before you leave.

Finally, they tell you who your target in this job is! This always comes of working for politically based organisations, they debate over everything, nothing gets done. As for the writer of the note - perhaps you met her last night? Who knows. You had a little too much to drink then, anything may have happened. Anyway, now: do the job, collect the money, and leave this stinking city by the shortest route available.

>destroy note
Simple enough - you rip it into tiny shreds, and drop these down the plughole in the sink.

>out

Corridor
The upstairs corridor of the inn is short enough, just leading to your room on the east and the other - west - which you presume is the landlord's own. The staircase in the corner runs down to the main room and the front desk. No other exits, no windows, no skylight. The staircase curves so that at the top you cannot see if anyone is coming up.

The door stands open.

>d
Sloppy - you forgot to lock your door. Annoyed with yourself, you wheel round, lock it, and wheel back, before going downstairs. What kind of assassin are you?

Inn
The stairs open out onto the main room of the inn. A long bar offering a multitude of bottles - which you sampled a fair few of last night - runs along one wall, above a smudged old mirror. A few customers sit at tables already, workers after the night-shifts and merchants enjoying the morning off. The landlord himself is firmly ensconsed, and waves a cheery greeting to you.

You return his greeting in kind, and seat yourself whilst he brings you breakfast - a pastry, with a strong black cup of coffee. You eat in silence, and quickly, and are soon ready to leave - no sign of the Guard. In fact, no sign of the Resistance either, whom you had better avoid until the job is complete. You are not stopped as you step outside.

The Resistance chose this place because of its perfect, backstreet location. No-one notices you, slipping through the alleyways, taking the quietest route to the Palace, a route you memorised on the boat yesterday. All is prepared. A man such as yourself needs no weapons.

Quiet Venetian Street
The alleyway is a typical shaded Venetian street, its walls barely a shoulder-width apart, rising three or four stories high on either side. The plaster is cream, and a few thin wiry balconies emerge on either side, dotted with flowers, looking small and crumpled in the shade.

The alley winds south to northeast, around the edges of the buildings deeper into the maze of Venice. In the pit of the alley's elbow, the southeast wall, there is a dark opening into which steps descend.

A metal sign on one wall reads: "Piazza St. Marco" and points along the alley southwards.

A little way south of the bend, at the bottom of the eastern wall, are two thin slits in the stone.

>s
You follow the alleyway along, until suddenly the buildings either side cease and you find yourself under a colonnade in the central Square of the City.

Piazza St. Marco
The square is magnificent - a wide expanse in which people mill, and enjoy the sunshine. All that surrounds them is gigantic, from the colossal pillars in the centre holding the statues of the city, to the wall of the Museum with its myriad windows running the perimeter, to the Palace itself, which stands to the east.

The thin alley-mouths dot the corners, under the colonnades, tucked away. Pigeons fly back and forth, smaller still than the people. The scaffold has been taken down.

>enter palace
You step into the shade of the Grand Staircase, which leads up to the Denizen's public rooms. A hush falls across your shoulders as your eyes adjust to the shade, and your mind to its purpose.

Grand Staircase
You pause to gaze at the surroundings in awe, even though it has not been so long since you were here last. The Staircase is wide enough to walk five men abreast, its stone of choice is polished, milky-brown marble. The roof curves overhead as though you were in a creature's throat, and is segmented into squares, each vividly painted with angels, cherubs, florets, portraits, landscapes, and so forth. Light trickles in from a window above and the Piazza below, meeting in the middle to join hands and spin.

From the corner of your eye you see the darkness has appeared. Now is not the time.

You feel a strange sensation, as though your eyes were dizzy.

>examine key
The key to your room.

At the top of the stairs a man emerges from the Denizen's room, grinning widely. He rushes down the steps past you, exclaiming: "They signed me up! I was going to hang tomorrow, but they've signed me up! I'm in the Army!" And he leaps out into the Square.

The thought passes across your head: That would be a way out of this.

>drop key
The stone steps are a light brown colour, and polished such that the sunlight makes needle-points on them.

>u
Notably, you are carrying:
  a room key

