wait

 ... go upstairs ... ceiling ... 


At the Top of the Grand Staircase
Here. Here he is. Giuseppe Florantine, your target; his door is just to the south. You can almost smell the kill, that little perfume of fear emitted before the end. And then, a fair payout, escape from this hole of a city, perhaps taking that blonde woman - whoever she may be, she must have been drinking with you last night - with you. Finally. An end to all this in sight, wrapped around Giuseppe Florantine's shattered neck.

Just below the window is a marble pedestal, on which is a large volume - the appointments book.
There is a strange sensation, again, stronger now. As though there were something else behind your eyeballs.

wait

 ... examine Giuseppe Florantine's door ... Giuseppe Florantine's door ... 

The door to this room is heavy wood, with a large brass keyhole. A plaque by the side reads: "OFFICE OF G. Florantine, AIDE TO THE DENIZEN".

>remove ropes
You can't see any such thing.

>listen
Thoughts - not your own - run around your head.

 ... knock on the Denizen's door ... the Denizen's door ... 

You hesitate - you ought to sign the book first.

>examine aide
You can't see any such thing.

>kill him
You can't see "him" (the Captain of the Guard) at the moment.

>i
No good. It's all no good. Helpless, you watch through your own eyes as your meeting with the Denizen plays out exactly as it had before. Though you see Giuseppe, standing so close you could reach out and snap his neck, something drags your feet away once more leaving you snapping at air. As you enter the Denizen's chamber, you see her ring resting on the table, by the candle, where it lay for the whole of your interview. You watch yourself taking it, just at a moment when the official looks away. You are unable to prevent it, and you feel the future drawing together as though knotted. You listen to yourself think it must have slid there.

"I was to be hanged - I was there, on the scaffold, in the Square; and just as the clock chimed the fifth chime I left it, stepped away." Not far enough, though, you realise that now.

There is only one brief moment of control through all this - as you sign the book, the movement is so automatic that you take command, and firmly write: "GIUSEPPE FLORANTINE IS A RESISTANCE MEMBER". A smile flicks across your mind, but not your face, as it is done.

Then finally you leave, back down the staircase. Florantine is so close once more - but you are not yours to control, and float away down the stairs, away from your target, into the Piazza. The scaffold is being erected as tomorrow morning is Friday. Friday is the day of the week when executions are performed.

Everything replays as before - the woman runs up to you: "I waited for you, like you told me to," she whines, her eyes loving you - you step down into the shaded passage - "If you weren't in your bed last night, you wouldn't have got it," she snarls, her eyes hating you.

And you are bound with rope, and tossed into the wine cellar, the key turning heavily in the lock. This time you are not listening close to that sound, and you hear below it a mutter, Francesca's voice to herself, coming to you once again in this cellar:

"And I shall tell the Guard, in case - in case, somehow" - her voice is sarcastic - "you get this key. They will know you by your ring."

Empty Room
It is the same as before. You perhaps, are not. A certain feel of control starts to seep back into your bones. Now - almost too late.

You wait a long while, voices waxing and waning outside the door. The only one you recognise is that of Francesca, who is waiting, it seems. Eventually she says: "I'm going for a walk, to stretch my legs." You hear her footsteps disappear down the alley.

Sunlight is gradually seeping through the windows.

>t aide
You can't see any such thing.

