Your arms are tied up, behind your back.

Footsteps approach along the alley, and into the passage. You hear a weasly voice say: "What the hell is this?"

He is met a long silence, until the leader replies. "What are you doing here?"

"One of your clowns left this," and paper rustles. "Do you want me hanged?"

>put ring on aide
You can't see any such thing.

>knock on door
You are not finished with Giuseppe.

The door opens, and you are thrown toward the wall. As you pick yourself up, you see a second man in your cell, also bound, and that man is Giuseppe Florantine, his face a pale mask of fear. He looks at you and quakes.

"Well, DeLosa, here he is!" exclaims the leader, slitting your bonds through with a deft flick of his knife. "All yours!"

"Leave me to it," you reply calmly.

"Knock when you are ready to leave, then." The leader steps out, and the door closes, the key turns.

"The Denizen's ring. She has sent you to kill me," whimpers Giuseppe.

>look

Empty Room
You stand, the only fixture in this small room. A little light has begun to seep through the crack windows over head; soon this room will be flooded. You feel a tension in the air.

Your head is buzzing slightly, but for now at least you are in control.

The aide is staring at you in utter fear.

>enter giuseppe
That's not something you can enter.

"Please, spare me!" wails Giuseppe.

>z. z
Time passes.

The aide is shivering with fright, his eyes staring.
Time passes.

"You are the assassin? No, please, no!" the aide pleads.

>remove ring
You slip the ill-fitting band off, and it glitters like a knife-point.

"Please, spare me!" wails Giuseppe.

>put ring on giuseppe
You turn the aide around, facing away, and he whimpers. You take his bound hand, and slowly slip on the ring, around his bony finger. He is sobbing, not trying to pull away, scared and trembling. "What are you doing?" he shrieks finally.

The aide is shivering with fright, his eyes staring.

>knock on door
You rap on the door, and the old man opens it. He looks curiously at Giuseppe, whimpering by one wall. "Is it done?"

"Yes," you nod, heading out. Suddenly, from nowhere, darkness springs up in that doorway. You try to avoid it, but that other thing in your mind is taking control once more and your feet are dragged toward the ink. You hear the leader speaking evil to the aide, and then your skin touches its edge and you feel yourself separate. One part of your mind steps into the black. The other, stays.

Then you watch yourself, as though from a birds-view, walk out of the door. You watch DeLosa who you know to be yourself leaving up the steps.

>z
You are just an eye and an ear, it seems.

What could I do? She told me to come here, told me I had to! If you stay, she said, I hang you, or they kill you, and there is no gain in either. This way there is chance of freedom, but no! I am being sacrificed! I should have fled!.. I could sell out, pass them things they do not know, about the Palace... Yes. Then they will spare me. But she would not.

Not your thoughts, not Giuseppe's voice, but the words echo around the room.

>z
You are just an eye and an ear, it seems.

>z
You are just an eye and an ear, it seems.

Suddenly you are gripped. Giuseppe flicks out of view, the world spins. Just as you slide you hear voices in the corridor outside - a man, Sebastian DeLosa, with your voice says: "Do not worry. You leave him there, I have sorted it all out."

"Are you sure?" asks the leader. "And it will not be traced here, to any of us? Are you sure of that?"

"Completely sure. Everything is done now. May I talk to your daughter later?" They leave, you hear their footsteps along the street. Seconds later, though it feels so much longer (your body is falling the whole while, making your stomach feel as though churned) a woman's footsteps approach along the street. "You are the guard?" Francesca asks.

"Yes, Miss."

"And our prisoner is secure?"

"Yes, Miss."

She approaches down the stairs.

It is too much to hold onto. Darkness envelops, Giuseppe drops to his knees. There is only one place left to go now, you think.


[Press any key]


Standing on tip-toes.


Scaffold in the City Square
Your head is pulled back, held by the rope pressing on your throat. Your toes pivot on a rickety stool, which shakes as your legs shake. The crowd filling the square are chattering like monkeys, but they are just flits of colour in the corners of your eyes. You cannot look round - instead you gaze straight across the Square, to the great dial of the Clock.

There is a hush in the Square, as the fifth chime dies away.

"We are here to hang..." exclaims the executioner from behind you. She is watching me! I can see her in the window, frowning dispassionate. I failed you! whispers something in your mind. Then another voice: We reach conclusion, friend. Forgive me if I have lied to you along the way. I cannot have your morals and your qualms affecting my work.

>z
Time passes.

The Captain steps nearer, and cries out: "Giuseppe Florantine! For the crime of: THIEVERY!" My name is William DeLosa, and I am an assassin. I near my rest once more, now. I thank you, Sebastian, one voice calmly utters. I am about to die! cries the other, far louder, far wilder.

>z
Time passes.

The Captain of the Guard comes up behind you and whispers: "I thought you were going to escape?" He laughs, but that is smothered by the sixth chime of the clock. The great sword-hand is pointing straight down, hanging as though the clock were limp.

There is a jolt as the stool is kicked away, and the rope grabs your neck, pulling upwards. You struggle to reach hold of it, but the manacles around your arms keep them down, and with every wriggle the fibres cut deeper into your throat. You cough and choke.

The sobbing, you notice, has stopped. Your eyes glimpse a woman, her blonde hair surrounds cold stone features.

The first ray of dawn slips over the edge of the building and as your body rotates it strikes your open eyes. A shadowing begins to grow. The last echo of the sixth chime shakes your body like a withered leaf.

And then as every morning the darkness swallows you, and you are powerless to prevent it.

  *** You have been hanged ***


[Press any key]


There is only one place for you, of course. In the crowd, watching the execution.


In the Crowd, Below the Scaffold
People surround you, pushing to see, baying like wolves. All except the old man by your side, and his daughter, who looks on dispassionately.

"I killed him," she says quietly, eventually.

"Yes," you reply. "Thank-you. But he would have died any other way."

She throws you a look of venom, as though to say: It was meant for you. But she says nothing.

"Come, Francesca," the old man says finally, as the body on the rope swings its final pirouette. "Let's be gone." To you he adds: "You arranged this all, somehow, didn't you though? What they say of you is true." You nod. "And can we be sure he really was a traitor? After all, they hanged him..."

"Quite sure," you reply. "But they would to fool you otherwise."

The three of you leave. You, to fill your pockets with gold, as ever, another job completed by the usual cycle. An assassin, such as yourself - you have no need of weapons.

Six o'clock comes, and conciousness fades.


    *** You rest ***



